<article class="article-layout">
    <header class="post-hero">
        <div class="post-hero-inner">
            <h1>I too, was fingerprinting everyone</h1>
            <time datetime="2026-05-19">19.05.2026</time>
        </div>
    </header>
    <div class="post-body">
        <p>And I stopped the moment I realized that it was creepy.</p>
<p>So there is this library that's called <strong>fingerprint.js</strong> that will do a neat trick for you: it produces a unique identifier based on the characteristics that the browsers leak to the javascript application, namely operating system, browser vendor, screen size, languages, and other elements.</p>
<p>Sure, you can also set cookies from the server, and do <em>other types of identification</em>, but this post is about the very act of identifying a user. Let's take the case of a website: a very simple act, which most consider harmless, usually justified with the &quot;we need to know the visitors we are getting to optimize their experience&quot; can quickly turn into a &quot;let's observe what everybody does in the website so we understand their behavior and ultimately, influence it&quot;.</p>
<hr />
<figure>
  <picture>
    <source type="image/webp" srcset="/moui-grove/assets/Piwik_screenshot_german-480w.webp 480w, /moui-grove/assets/Piwik_screenshot_german-960w.webp 960w, /moui-grove/assets/Piwik_screenshot_german-1440w.webp 1440w" sizes="(max-width: 600px) 100vw, 600px">
    <source type="image/png" srcset="/moui-grove/assets/Piwik_screenshot_german-480w.png 480w, /moui-grove/assets/Piwik_screenshot_german-960w.png 960w, /moui-grove/assets/Piwik_screenshot_german-1440w.png 1440w" sizes="(max-width: 600px) 100vw, 600px">
